</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="Ch4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4.1</span>: </span><span id="Ch4.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation results<span id="Ch4.T1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (SC09). For Poolformer, we report the final negative log-likelihood on the test set, with the best result (corresponding to the lowest validation loss) shown in parentheses.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="Ch4.S4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Normalization</h4>

<div id="Ch4.S4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We were not able to train the model without a normalization layer, even with careful initialization. Unlike <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, we find LayerNorm more beneficial than RMSNorm regarding model stability (see Figure <a href="#Ch4.F4" title="Figure 4.4 ‣ Normalization ‣ 4.4 Model Ablations ‣ Chapter 4 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ch4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="Ch4.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02206v1/norm-ll.svg" id="Ch4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/358;" width="476" height="358"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="Ch4.F4.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02206v1/norm-gr.svg" id="Ch4.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/358;" width="476" height="358"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="Ch4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4.4</span>: </span><span id="Ch4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of LayerNorm and RMSNorm<span id="Ch4.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (SC09). We find that LayerNorm makes training significantly more stable.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Ch4.S4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Initialization</h4>

<div id="Ch4.S4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our SkipBlock model, the residual stream gets contributions from the input, the residual blocks, and the up-pooling layer. In order to keep training stable, we initialize the last layers of the residual blocks and up-pooling layers sampling from a</p>
<table id="Ch4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ch4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\left(0,\frac{\mathtt{scale}}{n_{\mathrm{in}}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>𝚜𝚌𝚊𝚕𝚎</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\left(0,\frac{\mathtt{scale}}{n_{\mathrm{in}}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Ch4.S4.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of inputs. We try <math id="Ch4.S4.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{scale}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚜𝚌𝚊𝚕𝚎</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{scale}=0</annotation></semantics></math> (zero initialization), <math id="Ch4.S4.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{scale}=\frac{1}{n_{blocks}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚜𝚌𝚊𝚕𝚎</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{scale}=\frac{1}{n_{blocks}}</annotation></semantics></math> (our baseline), and <math id="Ch4.S4.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{scale}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚜𝚌𝚊𝚕𝚎</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{scale}=1</annotation></semantics></math> (Lecun normal initialization). The results are shown in Figure <a href="#Ch4.F5" title="Figure 4.5 ‣ Initialization ‣ 4.4 Model Ablations ‣ Chapter 4 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>: setting <math id="Ch4.S4.SSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{scale}\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚜𝚌𝚊𝚕𝚎</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{scale}\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math> is crucial for getting good performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ch4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02206v1/init-ll.svg" id="Ch4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/215;" width="286" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="Ch4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4.5</span>: </span><span id="Ch4.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of initialization scaling<span id="Ch4.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (SC09). Effect of scaling down the initialization variance of those layers whose outputs are added to the residual stream. Early in training, having a small scale is beneficial for stability, as the model has an identity-like behavior. In the long run, it seems to affect stability negatively. Interestingly, we see that setting <math id="Ch4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{scale}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚜𝚌𝚊𝚕𝚎</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{scale}=1</annotation></semantics></math> results in overfitting. </span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Ch4.S4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Gating</h4>

<div id="Ch4.S4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The gated variant of our residual block creates two branches, one with the MLP or temporal mixing layer, and another one with a dense layer (see Figure <a href="#Ch3.F4" title="Figure 3.4 ‣ 3.3 ResBlocks ‣ Chapter 3 Poolformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>). After this, both branches are multiplied component-wise and passed through a final dense layer. This results in significantly more parameters (7,272,704 instead of 5,489,408). As we can see in Figure <a href="#Ch4.F6" title="Figure 4.6 ‣ Gating ‣ 4.4 Model Ablations ‣ Chapter 4 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> the performance gains were quite modest. However, the stability greatly improves with gating.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ch4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="Ch4.F6.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02206v1/gating-ll.svg" id="Ch4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/358;" width="476" height="358"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="Ch4.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02206v1/gating-gr.svg" id="Ch4.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/358;" width="476" height="358"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="Ch4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4.6</span>: </span><span id="Ch4.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of gating<span id="Ch4.F6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (SC09). We found gating to be very beneficial for training stability. </span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Ch4.S4.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Skip-connection</h4>

<div id="Ch4.S4.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As we discussed in Figure <a href="#Ch3.F2" title="Figure 3.2 ‣ 3.1 SkipBlocks ‣ Chapter 3 Poolformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, there are two main ways of adding skip-connections in our model. Our experiments show that the best way is to add the residual connection around the pooling layers (short skip-connections).</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch4.S4.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This makes sense if we consider the alternative (long skip-connections), in which the skip-connection is added after the residual blocks. In the outer-most layer, the input would be copied directly to the output, which means that the final residual blocks would have to first cancel out the features from the original input before adding their own contribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ch4.S4.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">ResBlocks position</h4>

<div id="Ch4.S4.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can place residual blocks before or after the pooling layers (see Figure <a href="#Ch3.F2" title="Figure 3.2 ‣ 3.1 SkipBlocks ‣ Chapter 3 Poolformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>). In this experiment, we consider two alternatives: having residual blocks both before and after, and having residual blocks just after the pooling layers. To make the comparison fair, we make the total number of RG-LRU layers the same.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch4.S4.SSx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We show that placing all of them after the pooling results in a slight increase in performance: the test negative log-likelihood goes down from 1.854 to 1.851. We hypothesize that the reason for this is that most of the short-term processing is performed in the last layers, so placing 8 layers instead of 4 after the last up-pooling layer results in an increase in performance. See Figure <a href="#Ch4.F9" title="Figure 4.9 ‣ Depth-wise Analysis ‣ 4.5 Pooling Experiments ‣ Chapter 4 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a> for more information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ch4.S4.SSx6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Attention in the deepest layer</h4>

<div id="Ch4.S4.SSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we replace the four RG-LRU layers in the deepest level with multi-query attention (4 heads). The reason to place the self-attention layer there is that the sequence length is just <math id="Ch4.S4.SSx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{16000}{2\cdot 4\cdot 4\cdot 5}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>16000</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{16000}{2\cdot 4\cdot 4\cdot 5}=100</annotation></semantics></math>, so it is computationally feasible. This results in a slight increase in performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ch4.S4.SSx7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Complex numbers</h4>

<div id="Ch4.S4.SSx7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="Ch4.S4.SSx7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> parameter in RG-LRU layers can be real or complex. Recent research has shown that the complex parametrization is not useful for language modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. In our case, it seems that for audio modeling, the complex parametrization slightly outperforms the real one.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch4.S4.SSx7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As explained in Section <a href="#Ch3.S4" title="3.4 Temporal Mixing Layer ‣ Chapter 3 Poolformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, <math id="Ch4.S4.SSx7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is parametrized to lie within the unit circle in the complex plane, and we expect most of the values to be close to the boundary. It is interesting to consider several initialization schemes</p>
<ul id="Ch4.S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ch4.S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch4.S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch4.S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Small ring (baseline):</span> sample the magnitude from <math id="Ch4.S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\mathcal{U}[0.9^{2},0.99^{2}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mn>0.9</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>0.99</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\mathcal{U}[0.9^{2},0.99^{2}]}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the phase from <math id="Ch4.S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}[0,\frac{\pi}{10}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}[0,\frac{\pi}{10}]</annotation></semantics></math>, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch4.S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch4.S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch4.S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Half ring:</span> sample the magnitude from <math id="Ch4.S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\mathcal{U}[0.9^{2},0.99^{2}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mn>0.9</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>0.99</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\mathcal{U}[0.9^{2},0.99^{2}]}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the phase from <math id="Ch4.S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}[0,\pi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}[0,\pi]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch4.S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch4.S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch4.S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full ring</span> sample the magnitude from <math id="Ch4.S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\mathcal{U}[0.9^{2},0.99^{2}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mn>0.9</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>0.99</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\mathcal{U}[0.9^{2},0.99^{2}]}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the phase from <math id="Ch4.S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}[0,2\pi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}[0,2\pi]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch4.S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch4.S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch4.S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full circle</span> sample the magnitude from <math id="Ch4.S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\mathcal{U}[0.01^{2},0.99^{2}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mn>0.01</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>0.99</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\mathcal{U}[0.01^{2},0.99^{2}]}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the phase from <math id="Ch4.S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}[0,2\pi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}[0,2\pi]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Ch4.S4.SSx7.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#Ch4.F7" title="Figure 4.7 ‣ Complex numbers ‣ 4.4 Model Ablations ‣ Chapter 4 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a> we can visually see the <math id="Ch4.S4.SSx7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> values at initialization, and after training. In Table <a href="#Ch4.T1" title="Table 4.1 ‣ 4.4 Model Ablations ‣ Chapter 4 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> we observe similar results as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>: reducing the initialization range of the phase seems crucial for capturing long-range dependencies.</p>
</div>
