<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="Ch4.T5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4.5</span>: </span><span id="Ch4.T5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison with state-of-the-art models<span id="Ch4.T5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (SC09).</span></span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="Ch4.T5.7" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">We don’t use rejection sampling to compute the perceptual metrics.</p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="Ch4.S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#Ch4.T6" title="Table 4.6 ‣ 4.7 Comparison with the State of the Art ‣ Chapter 4 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> compares the negative log-likelihood of these models on the Beethoven and YouTubeMix datasets. For the Beethoven dataset we used a pooling configuration of <math id="Ch4.S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2,4,4,5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2,4,4,5]</annotation></semantics></math>, a layer configuration of <math id="Ch4.S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[8,4,4,4,4]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[8,4,4,4,4]</annotation></semantics></math>, and a recurrence dimension of 512. We train the model for 66K steps, and got a negative log-likelihood of 0.915, which is significantly better than SaShiMi, which was trained for 1M steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch4.S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch4.S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For YouTubeMix, we used a pooling configuration of <math id="Ch4.S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2,2,4,4]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2,2,4,4]</annotation></semantics></math>, a layer configuration of <math id="Ch4.S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[8,4,4,4,4]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[8,4,4,4,4]</annotation></semantics></math>, and a recurrence dimension of 512. We trained the model for 10K steps and obtained a negative log-likelihood of 1.391. While this is worse than SaShiMi’s result, it’s worth noting that their model was trained for 600K steps. Additionally, our model operates on full 1-minute sequences, whereas SaShiMi is trained on shorter 8-second chunks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ch4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Ch4.T6.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
